<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <ScenTitle>Trapped Under Ice, 1999</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Original Concept By:&lt;/B&gt; Dimitris Dranidis&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Date:&lt;/B&gt; June 15th 1999 &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Location:&lt;/B&gt; Arctic Ice Cap &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Playable Side:&lt;/B&gt; United States &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Duration:&lt;/B&gt; 2 Days&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Since the early 1980s, the Soviet/Russian Navy has been using under-ice locations as patrol areas for its SSBN force, typically employing its most modern and ice-capable submarines such as the Typhoon &amp;amp; Delta-IV classes. Far away from NATO's traditional ASW surveillance areas, these areas offer additional challenges for effective strategic ASW since they are accessible only by submarines, rendering NATO's all-arms ASW strategy ineffective and (in the marginal areas) they severely degrade sonar performance because of the drift ice.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;In this scenario, the Pristina airport crisis in the immediate aftermath of operation Allied Force has rapidly escalated. NATO forces have clashed with dug-in Russian troops in the airport, with high casualties on both sides. General Jackson's grim warning ("I'm not going to start the Third World War for you") has not been heeded and the situation is indeed careening towards a full-tilt strategic confrontation. As an ultimate warning, Washington decides to demonstrate to the Russian political leadership the vulnerability of its supposedly "secure" retaliatory strike force. Among other actions, this means attacking Russian SSBNs deep under the polar ice.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
